<description>
For operator-fill problems, use Python's Fraction (from fractions module) instead of floats to avoid floating-point precision errors when comparing results to integer targets. This ensures exact equality checks, especially important when division produces repeating decimals.
</description>
<example>
```python
from fractions import Fraction
from itertools import product

a, b, c = 19, 8, 28
operators = ['+', '-', '*', '/']
target = Fraction(-205)

for op1, op2 in product(operators, repeat=2):
    try:
        result = Fraction(a)
        # apply ops left to right with precedence
        # (simplified: for 3 numbers, handle * / first)
        if op1 in ('*', '/') and op2 in ('*', '/'):
            result = Fraction(a)
            if op1 == '*': result *= b
            else: result /= b
            if op2 == '*': result *= c
            else: result /= c
        elif op1 in ('*', '/') and op2 in ('+', '-'):
            result = Fraction(a)
            if op1 == '*': result *= b
            else: result /= b
            if op2 == '+': result += c
            else: result -= c
        elif op1 in ('+', '-') and op2 in ('*', '/'):
            if op1 == '*': result = a * b
            elif op1 == '/': result = Fraction(a, b)
            else:
                result = a - b
                if op2 == '*': result *= c
                else: result /= c
        else:
            result = Fraction(a)
            if op1 == '+': result += b
            else: result -= b
            if op2 == '+': result += c
            else: result -= c
        if result == target:
            print(f"{a} {op1} {b} {op2} {c} = {result}")
    except ZeroDivisionError:
        pass
```
